viogpuap: look past display devices of other cards for virtio-gpu's.

The resolution helper (viogpuap, started in the console session by the
vgpusrv service) finds virtio-gpu's display devices by walking
EnumDisplayDevices, but FindDisplayDevice returned FALSE at the first
device whose ID did not match, which ended FindAdapters' loop.  On a
machine with another card enumerated first (2ksbox's keep the standard
VGA on x64, ramfb on Arm, for setup and recovery) it found no adapter at
all, so a new size from the host never reached the desktop.  It now walks
every display device until one matches.  Measured on x64 Windows 11: the
desktop follows the player window again within QEMU's one-second settle.

diff --git a/viogpu/viogpuap/GpuAdaptersMgr.cpp b/viogpu/viogpuap/GpuAdaptersMgr.cpp
index 34111b6..19f93e8 100644
--- a/viogpu/viogpuap/GpuAdaptersMgr.cpp
+++ b/viogpu/viogpuap/GpuAdaptersMgr.cpp
@@ -56,17 +56,23 @@ BOOL GpuAdaptersMgr::FindDisplayDevice(PDISPLAY_DEVICE lpDisplayDevice, std::wst
 {
     PrintMessage(L"%ws\n", __FUNCTIONW__);
 
-    DWORD index = *adapterIndex;
-    (*adapterIndex)++;
-
-    if (GetDisplayDevice(NULL, index, lpDisplayDevice, 0))
+    // every display device until one matches, not only the next: the first
+    // is often another card's (2ksbox's machines keep the standard VGA, or
+    // ramfb on Arm, beside virtio-gpu), which ended the search at once
+    for (;;)
     {
+        DWORD index = *adapterIndex;
+        (*adapterIndex)++;
+
+        if (!GetDisplayDevice(NULL, index, lpDisplayDevice, 0))
+        {
+            return FALSE;
+        }
         if (!name.empty() && !_wcsnicmp(lpDisplayDevice->DeviceID, name.c_str(), name.size()))
         {
             return TRUE;
         }
     }
-    return FALSE;
 }
 
 BOOL GpuAdaptersMgr::Init()
